- Arrays vs ArrayLists (by Karan Dabas) -
-----------------------------------------

“Code is like humor, when you have to explain it, it’s bad” - Cory House

----------------------------
	Introduction
-----------------------------

In this section, we will look into the key properties of Array and ArrayList, where they differ from each other and when to use 
them. We will also answer common questions from students on this topic. Apart from that, we will also look into time 
complexities, like how much time does it take to perform certain actions (add, delete and update) given n elements in an Array 
or ArrayList. In the end, we will dive into common algorithms around this topic like sorting, and searching for elements in 
an Array or ArraList.


---------------------------------------------------
	Properties of an Array and ArrayList
---------------------------------------------------

We will look into the structure, type, and operations of Array and ArrayList. To point out that both Arrays and ArrayLists are 
examples of what we call data structures. It means a structure of some kind that holds data/information in it. 

An Array is a collection of elements that are of the same data type and are stored in a contiguous memory location. This means 
that we can access the next element from the current position by simply moving to the adjacent memory location. To create an 
Array we need to know two things, size and data type. The size defines the maximum number of elements that we can store in a 
contiguous memory block and the data type tells us what type of data we can store in an Array. The syntax for defining an Array 
is shown below.

An ArrayList is also a collection of elements of some type (must be non-primitive reference types), at a first look, one might see that an ArrayList is similar to an Array but the reality is quite different. An ArrayList is dynamic in size which 
means that it does not require a predefined size constraint and we can add elements as many times as we want. The syntax for 
defining an ArrayList is shown below.

Now let’s explore some differences between Arrays and ArrayLists. And what kind of operations can we perform on them.

---------------------------------------------------
	What Can it Store?
---------------------------------------------------

Arrays can store any type. This includes both primitive types (like int, double, char, etc.) and non-primitive types 
(like String and custom created classes).

ArrayLists can only store non-primitive types (like String and custom created classes, and also wrapper class types like 
Integer and Double

---------------------------------------------------
	How are Element Accessed?
---------------------------------------------------

Both Arrays and ArrayLists provide access to individual elements by index. Arrays use the square brackets ([ ]), and 
ArrayLists use their get and set methods for accessing elements.

--------------------------------------------------
	Is it Fixed Size?
--------------------------------------------------

While Arrays are fixed in size, and ArrayList allows adding inserting, and removing elements with the add, and remove methods.

--------------------------------------------------
	What Else Can it Do?
--------------------------------------------------

Arrays can tell us their size (using the length attribute), and allow us to store and retrieve a fixed number of element values. 
ArrayLists can do all this and much more (Review the Method Summary section in this document for more detials 
- https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/ArrayList.html).

Even though both Array and ArrayList can perform the same basic operations, the underlying implementation to do the same is 
quite different. We will talk more about this in detail in the time complexity section below. The Arrays does not have a 
built-in delete or add operation but we can still achieve similar results to that of an ArrayList using an algorithm listed in 
the last section.

-------------------------------------------------
	When to use Array and ArrayList?
-------------------------------------------------

Use Arrays when you know the number of elements needed to perform certain tasks, and the number of element will not change 
while the program is running. The ArrayList is used for the cases when you don’t know how many elements you may end up adding 
to the data structure in advance. 

